# Deployed by migration 000586-wacom-tablet.sh -- do not edit by hand.
#
# Hides the Wacom PAD device (the ExpressKey buttons on the tablet body)
# from libinput -- and therefore from the Wayland session entirely. The pen
# and eraser nodes are untouched: only devices whose name ends in " Pad" are
# matched, and hid-wacom registers stylus and pad as separate input nodes
# with one USB id.
#
# Why: the compositor exposes every tablet pad to its Wayland clients as a
# zwp_tablet_pad_v2, and GTK 4 then delivers a GDK_PAD_BUTTON_PRESS to every
# GTK4 app in the session -- built with the app's keyboard-focus surface,
# which is NULL in any app without focus. gdk_surface_handle_event()
# dereferences it unguarded (gdksurface.c:2881), so one pad-button press
# segfaults every unfocused GTK4 app (GTK #8298; ghostty 2026-11-27, one
# process hosting all terminals). GTK 4.24.2 ships the bug and no release
# fixes it. GTK3 apps only warn (the matching 'GDK_IS_DEVICE (device)'
# assertion spam in xdg-desktop-portal-gtk logs).
#
# Nothing of value is lost: no Wayland client ever consumed pad events
# usefully (Hyprland has no tablet-button binds, verified against the
# binary, and Xournal++/browsers ignore them), which is exactly why
# hypr-tablet-buttons exists -- it reads the raw evdev node directly, and
# a libinput-ignore does not affect raw evdev access. Removing this rule
# once a fixed gtk4 is current makes the pad visible to Wayland again.

SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", ATTRS{name}=~"Wacom .* Pad", ENV{LIBINPUT_IGNORE_DEVICE}="1"
